hallenges the traditional view of proteins as simple molecular machines and suggests instead that they exhibit a basic form of \"intelligence\". The idea stems from suggestions coming from Integrated Information Theory (IIT), network theory, and allostery to explore how proteins process information, adapt to their environment, and even show memory-like behaviors. We define protein intelligence using IIT and focus on how proteins integrate information (in terms of the parameter Φ coming from IIT) and balance their core (stable, ordered regions) and periphery (flexible, disordered regions). This balance allows proteins to remain stable while adapting to changes and operating in a critical state where order and disorder coexist. We summarize recent findings on conformational memory, allosteric regulation, protein intrinsic disorder, liquid-liquid phase separation, and critical transitions, and compare protein behavior

